BT /F2 14 Tf 0.01 0.43 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 50 713.0 Tm (HISTORICAL CHRONOLOGY) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 679.0 Tm (Ext3 was developed by Stephen Tweedie in the late 1990s, with kernel documentation) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 662.0 Tm (identifying a September 1999 release. The project built on ext2 instead of introducing an) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 645.0 Tm (incompatible new layout. Its journalling changes entered mainline Linux in the 2.4-era,) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 628.0 Tm (helping Linux systems avoid the expensive whole-volume consistency scans that could follow) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 611.0 Tm (a crash on an ext2 installation.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 582.0 Tm (The conversion story was central to adoption: ext3 can add a journal to an ext2-formatted) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 565.0 Tm (volume without wholesale copying every file. The resulting shared heritage made it) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 548.0 Tm (practical for distributions to move to journalling while retaining recovery tools and) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 531.0 Tm (established ext2 metadata structures. Ext4 later superseded ext3 for many modern workloads) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 514.0 Tm (while retaining support for a compatible subset.) Tj ET
