BT /F2 14 Tf 0.01 0.43 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 50 713.0 Tm (ALLOCATION AND DIRECTORIES) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 679.0 Tm (Ext3 uses block and inode bitmaps for each group, with locality-aware allocation policy) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 662.0 Tm (inherited from ext2. Regular file data is mapped through inode pointers and indirect) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 645.0 Tm (blocks. Directory entries contain inode numbers, record lengths, name lengths and bytes of) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 628.0 Tm (the name. Larger directory optimisations, including HTree indexing where supported, change) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 611.0 Tm (lookup behaviour without abandoning the fundamental inode namespace.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 582.0 Tm (Hard-link counts, metadata ordering and atomic rename semantics must be read together with) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 565.0 Tm (journal operation. A directory tree is not recovered merely by seeing a committed journal) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 548.0 Tm (record; all referenced blocks and feature-specific metadata must be interpretable and) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 531.0 Tm (within bounds. The journalling layer protects an ordered set of modifications rather than) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 514.0 Tm (replacing all underlying format constraints.) Tj ET
