BT /F2 14 Tf 0.01 0.43 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 50 713.0 Tm (INTEGRITY AND RECOVERY) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 679.0 Tm (JBD logs transactions that can be replayed after a crash. The familiar write modes differ:) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 662.0 Tm (journal mode can journal file data as well as metadata, ordered mode orders data relative) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 645.0 Tm (to associated metadata commits, and writeback provides weaker data ordering. It is) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 628.0 Tm (incorrect to say ext3 always journals every file byte. A successful journal replay repairs) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 611.0 Tm (many incomplete metadata transactions, but cannot replace checks of damaged sectors or) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 594.0 Tm (corrupted inode maps.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 565.0 Tm (The recovery process identifies committed transactions and replays appropriate metadata) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 548.0 Tm (updates before presenting the filesystem as consistent. If journal structures are torn or) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 531.0 Tm (corrupt, e2fsck remains necessary. Write barriers and stable-storage assumptions are) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 514.0 Tm (essential to interpreting a commit as durable. Journal existence does not protect against) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 497.0 Tm (malicious or concurrent offline modification.) Tj ET
